# M6 Release-Candidate Lessons

Status: **complete; owner closeout acceptance pending**

## What worked

- Building one wheel and reusing its exact bytes across installation, example, performance, and
  procedure jobs removed ambiguity about what each cell validated.
- Keeping claims, checks, evidence, verification, and owner acceptance separate prevented green CI
  or worker assertions from silently becoming release authority.
- Static examples plus executable fresh-user rehearsals covered both inspectable documentation and
  actual installed behavior.
- The capability-free, data-only dogfood workflow proved that FORGE can govern its own framework
  changes without granting the worker acceptance or publication power.
- Exact security inventories, preserved report digests, and path-constrained secret exceptions
  made remediation reviewable rather than relying on an unexplained scanner bypass.

## What should improve

- Run representative hosted performance cells earlier whenever runner images, supported Python
  versions, or budgets change. Deferring all remote CI saved increment time but concentrated
  environment discovery in closeout.
- Keep performance workloads isolated locally and remotely; concurrent rehearsals can invalidate
  comparisons even when product behavior is unchanged.
- Upgrade GitHub Actions to releases that natively target the current Node.js runtime before hosted
  enforcement turns annotations into failures.
- Periodically exercise Windows symbolic-link cases in a deliberately privileged environment while
  retaining explicit skips on ordinary hosts.
- Repeat dependency, vulnerability, license, and secret evidence for every changed release
  candidate and immediately before any public package publication.

## Guidance for separately authorized M7 planning

Before M7 implementation, define its increments and owner gates explicitly. Planning should address
version finalization, release notes, action-runtime maintenance, fresh security evidence, artifact
signing and provenance, tag creation, package-index upload, rollback, and post-publication support.
These are recommendations only; this M6 record authorizes none of those actions.
